[
    {
        "content": "<p>Aristotle was able to write a nice computational proof of where the first nontrivial zero of the Riemann zeta function is. Specifically, proving that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is exactly one zero with imaginary part between 0 and 15</li>\n<li>That zero has imaginary part between 14 and 15. (It actually proved between 14.0 and 14.2)</li>\n<li>That zero has real part of 1/2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And since Aristotle now supports shareable links, here is my exact conversation!<br>\nThis was <a href=\"https://aristotle.harmonic.fun/dashboard/requests/964b679d-ed5d-4a8c-9f86-def3b052e2c4\">the first batch</a>, and <a href=\"https://aristotle.harmonic.fun/dashboard/requests/2c69590a-b50b-40f7-b6d0-e434ba071d42\">the second</a>.</p>\n<p>Relevant theorem statements:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemannZeta_unique_nontrivial_zero_below_15</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∃!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemannZeta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">im</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">im</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">15</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first_nontrivial_zero_of_riemannZeta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">14</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">15</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemannZeta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemannZeta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">14</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">im</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemannZeta_unique_zero_14_15</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∃!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">14</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">15</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">riemannZeta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>These are clearly a little redundant, but depending on what you want I think they're all \"reasonable\" statements.</p>\n<p>This number is actually relevant -- knowing that zeta doesn't have zeros with a smaller imaginary value sets bounds on accuracy of some prime number estimates. Sadly, I don't think any of this code could go into Mathlib (no matter the quantity of human cleanup), because the heavy computations rely on native_decide quite extensively.</p>",
        "id": 605393426,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782056109
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is nice, you should at least post it to Github somewhere if you haven't already.  Its possible the PNT project might want results like this at some point.</p>",
        "id": 605410467,
        "sender_full_name": "Alastair Irving",
        "timestamp": 1782062487
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"448405\">@Alex Meiburg</span> What do you need the native_decide for? Some form of numeric computations, I assume?</p>",
        "id": 605676219,
        "sender_full_name": "Johan Commelin",
        "timestamp": 1782125947
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah, finding (resp. not finding) the zero in some range of values is done by evaluating some polynomial approximations to real functions and showing that they cross from positive to negative (resp. they are too large compared to the error term, to cross zero)</p>",
        "id": 605679541,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782126648
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I admit I haven't properly tried vanilla decide. I should, I'll see how fast or not it is</p>",
        "id": 605679650,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782126670
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>Sadly, I don't think any of this code could go into Mathlib (no matter the quantity of human cleanup), because the heavy computations rely on native_decide quite extensively.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So that means that if we get some sort of interval arithmetic working, then this would get unblocked?<br>\nBecause <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"223948\">David Ledvinka</span> has a draft PR on interval arithmetic.</p>",
        "id": 605719045,
        "sender_full_name": "Johan Commelin",
        "timestamp": 1782134664
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is it possible to give a self-contained \"baby example\" of the kind of problem where you have used <code>native_decide</code> -- something which imports only mathlib, is representative of what you're doing, and for which other techniques time out?</p>",
        "id": 605719638,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1782134774
    },
