        "id": 606042149,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782230598
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ruling out zeros below 14 requires evaluating recursively bisecting the range [0,14] into small enough pieces where you can evaluate the whole interval, get some error bounds, and bound it away from zero. That's just, kind of expensive, unless you're very clever and careful with the evaluation methods.</p>\n<p>That's what the <code>sweepB</code> does: all the rest of the code builds up the formula that it will evaluate to bound a given interval (<code>chkI tlo thi</code>), and then sweepB tries chkI on a given interval and, if it returns false, tries recursively goes down to the next step, up to some provided fuel. native_decide runs this and then we have a certificate.</p>",
        "id": 606042437,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782230695
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think that Aristotle here used something \"reasonably\" intelligent for evaluation, but I'm sure there's lots of room for optimizing the formulas used.</p>",
        "id": 606042495,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782230709
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If someone wanted to try to make this more efficient, to the point of being kernel reducible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For one thing I'm sure there's a lot of room for picking better formulas for what function to evaluate. The closer to constant the better. :)</li>\n<li>There are \"smarter\" ways to bound the values on an interval, like if you group the terms together so that more errors cancel. For example, if you want to bound cos(x) on the interval [12,13], then you'll get very bad bounds by first doing the Taylor series terms and then combining them. But you can get better error with the same degree of approximation if you group the Taylor series terms together differently so that the high powers are small. (Essentially, doing a midpoint evaluation.)</li>\n<li>There are probably lots of aspects of the precisions of relative terms that are far from optimized. Controlling how many terms on one series vs. another would probably spot a lot of wasted effort.</li>\n<li>There may be spots where a hard-coded 'certificate' can save a lot of effort. Like, instead of letting it adaptively bisect the intervals, or retrying things with higher precision, specifying in the Lean code which intervals to evaluate with which precisions.</li>\n<li>There are tricks to speed up just the raw interval math. Using exclusively dyadic rationals can shrink the numbers a lot at a controllable, small, loss in precision.</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 606043842,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1782231061
    }
]